import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"
pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Construct an invalid-size reason carrying the supplied integer.",
  taskTitle: "How do I construct and read a size reason?",
  purpose: "NonPositiveSize is a constructor: a name that creates a value. It packages a supplied integer as a SizeError. When chunksOf returns an invalid-size reason, use that name to read the number and explain what failed.",
  setup: "The imports give std/array and std/collection the local names arrays and collections. In fn batches size: Int -> values: Array<Int> -> String = ..., fn introduces the function name batches. The function receives size, then values; the first arrow separates those arguments and the final -> String gives its text result type. Int means an integer, and <Int> chooses the Array element type. The expression after = supplies the returned value. Calls separate arguments with spaces; parentheses group an inner call's result as one argument to another call.",
  codeTitle: "Read batching results and invalid-size reasons",
  outputTitle: "Seseragi output",
  after: [
    "In batches, size: Int is the requested integer size and values: Array<Int> is an array of integers. -> String says the function returns text. arrays.chunksOf size values receives the size first, then the array, and returns either the groups or a failure reason.",
    "The returned type is Either<SizeError, Array<Array<Int>>>. Left problem selects failure and names the contained SizeError problem. Right groups selects success and names the contained array of arrays groups. match chooses between these named alternatives and returns the expression after ->. No exception is thrown by returning Left.",
    "describe receives a SizeError. The collections.NonPositiveSize size branch names its contained integer size. This is currently the only reason in SizeError, so the branch handles every alternative. show size turns that integer into text, and + joins it to the explanation.",
    "Size two splits [10, 20, 30] into [[10, 20], [30]], keeping the shorter final group. With the same size, empty input gives []. Zero and -2 instead return a reason carrying the requested number. The zero-size example fails even with empty input; emptiness does not bypass size checking.",
    "The final collections.NonPositiveSize 2 constructs a value even with a positive integer and prints NonPositiveSize 2. This constructor only packages an integer; it does not check its sign. chunksOf and windows are the operations that validate their requested sizes.",
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Compare the same task in TypeScript",
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript can also declare a small result type for successful groups or a failure reason. | permits either object shape; the string in kind selects which shape it is. Only success has groups and only failure has problem, so checking kind tells the caller which property it can use. SizeError names the shape carrying the reason and its size number.",
  comparisonAfter: "A TypeScript for loop and slice implement the same batching task. Both programs return failure for nonpositive sizes and retain the final short group. TypeScript uses JSON for arrays, so its spacing differs from Seseragi display. The last TypeScript line shows the reason object as JSON, where Seseragi displays NonPositiveSize 2. Both allow this positive payload: defining the reason type is separate from validating an input.",
  detailTitle: "Type guarantees and common mistakes",
  details: [
    "SizeError names the reason type; NonPositiveSize constructs a value of that type. An annotation is not a conversion: let problem: collections.SizeError = 0 produces SES-T0101. If you intend to construct a reason directly, write collections.NonPositiveSize 0; in ordinary processing, handle the Left returned by chunksOf.",
    "In NonPositiveSize arg1: Int -> SizeError, arg1: Int means one integer argument and -> SizeError means the returned reason value. Its type contains no restriction to nonpositive numbers. The opaque-type classification hides internal representation; the public NonPositiveSize constructor still lets you construct a reason and read its integer with match.",
    "The comparison uses only small integers. TypeScript number also permits fractions, which this task does not compare. Successful batching visits the input and builds its result, requiring work and storage proportional to the number of elements. An invalid size is returned before batching begins.",
  ],
}
